export const STRENGTH_WORKFLOW_RESOURCE = `# Strength-first workflow

${STRENGTH_CORE_WORKFLOW}

For a tongue-and-groove joint, use the tongue-root method only as a limited component screen when its beam idealization and orientation-matched material properties are supported. It never validates the full joint; report groove contact and mating-part checks as unresolved.

The tools cover nominal rectangular members, bounded separate and integral rectangular-panel geometry for one plate method, one bounded single-through-fastener plate case, fastener-member and threaded-receiver axial screening, a root-only rectangular tongue screen, one heat-set-insert retention case, elastic in-plane fastener-group load distribution, exact planar sections under supported idealizations, and linear-elastic C3D4 static FEA cases for a single Solid with explicit per-face global translational restraints, including optional layerwise orthotropic response for one measured material on complete confirmed G-code layers with perfectly bonded interfaces. Legacy support faces remain fully fixed; selectively fixed x/y/z directions are accepted only when grounded in the real support condition. FEA cases are independent scenarios on byte-identical meshes; optional mesh refinement reports changes between up to three resolutions as trend evidence, not a convergence verdict. The free-moment model distributes an equivalent nodal couple across the selected face, so interpret local stress accordingly. A section may come from an existing face or from a temporary native cut on an arbitrary plane; neither path uses a render mesh as measurement evidence. Solver output is not a strength pass and does not replace a structural code, test data, complete joint design, buckling analysis, fatigue analysis, impact analysis, thermal analysis, or qualified engineering review. A geometric verification proves only the selected native B-rep geometry and current Plasticity identity. Nominal section, plate or fastener stress is not whole-part validation.

## Worked example: known dimensions

The user provides an 80 × 20 × 6 mm cantilever, a 40 N tip load, a 60 mm attachment distance and a 1 mm displacement limit. Record each value with units and provenance. If the exact printed material/profile/orientation properties are missing, return a conditional material question before treating the result as a pass. Calculate the supported cantilever scenario, propose one logical thickness change if required, wait for acceptance unless the task was explicitly delegated, then read the actual native geometry and recalculate.

## Worked example: photo without scale

A photo shows a phone stand but contains no scale. Find an accessible primary source for the named device mass and dimensions, record the URL and uncertainty, and ask for the attachment distance and mounting condition. Convert mass to force only with explicit gravity evidence. Evaluate explicit candidate dimensions. Do not extract an exact length from the photo and do not modify Plasticity until the required facts and task scope are resolved.

## Unsupported ribbed part

A ribbed, hollow, tapered or perforated part is not a constant solid-equivalent rectangular member. Return an unsupported outcome with the observed exclusions. Do not substitute its outer bounds for a rectangular section.

## Rectangular enclosure panel

For a separate flat rectangular lid or an integral rectangular enclosure wall under known uniform normal pressure, first resolve clear spans, net pressure, Young's modulus, Poisson ratio, material limits, allowable centre deflection and the actual condition of all four edges. For an integral wall, inspect two opposed planar faces on the same Solid with \`plasticity_inspect_integral_rectangular_plate\`; use \`plasticity_verify_integral_plate_strength\` to replace supplied spans and thickness with measured B-rep values. The inspector supports a plain rectangular wall panel only; its midpoint span is derived from the two face outlines and measured wall thickness. Neither tool infers pressure or support. Use \`simply-supported-plate-uniform-pressure-v1\` only when all four edges are explicitly confirmed as ideal simple supports and the panel has constant thickness with no openings, ribs, bosses or curvature. A clipped lid or screwed cover is not automatically simply supported. Keep edge fixity, local fastener load transfer, printed anisotropy and whole-enclosure behavior visible as unchecked.

## Worked planar rectangle

For a 20 × 10 mm planar face, exact native boundaries give area 200 mm², centroid [10, 5] mm in the local frame, Ixx = 1666.6666666667 mm⁴ and Iyy = 6666.6666666667 mm⁴. Show the selected face, frame, forces, moments and unchecked scopes in Codex chat before calculating. The scenario tool treats caller geometry as unverified; the verification tool re-reads the face and replaces all section properties with native measurements.

## Holed section

A centred Ø4 mm hole in that rectangle gives area 200 − 4π mm² and subtracts 4π mm⁴ from each centroidal second moment. Nominal axial and bending stress may still be shown, but the result stays conditional because local stress concentration and net-section fracture are unchecked. Nonzero direct shear is unsupported because a perforated rectangle is outside the proven shear families. A true concentric circular annulus is supported, but its inner boundary still keeps local stress concentration and net-section fracture conditional.

## Single loaded fastener

For one through fastener transferring in-plane load into a rectangular plate, select both broad opposed faces and provide the actual in-plane load direction. Inspect exact native geometry before verification. Supply separate traceable bearing, shear and tensile limits for the current material, printer profile and orientation. The method checks plate bearing, loaded-edge shear-out and net-section tension; it does not check the fastener, preload, slip, clearance, fatigue, prying, pull-through, inserts, threads or a fastener group. Keep 1.5 ≤ e/d < 2 conditional and reject this simplified passport below 1.5.

## Fastener member

Use \`fastener-member-v1\` after the load reaching one physical fastener is known. Record the nominal diameter, tensile stress area, effective area of each actual shear plane, whether one or two equal planes carry shear, and whether the plane crosses the smooth shank or threads. Areas and tensile/shear limits need traceable sources for the selected grade and condition. \`plasticity_calculate_fastener_member_strength\` checks axial tension, direct shear and the NASA screening interaction \`R_t² + R_s³ ≤ 1\`. The axial input must include applicable preload. Keep bolt bending, joint slip and redistribution, thread/insert pull-out, joined-member failure, fatigue and critical-use validation explicit as unchecked.

## Tongue root

Use \`tongue-root-transverse-v1\` only for a rectangular prismatic tongue root that can reasonably be idealized as a cantilever with one transverse point load and a known lever arm. Confirm that beam kinematics apply. Supply traceable root width, bending thickness, lever arm, force, orientation-matched effective Young's and shear moduli, tensile and shear allowables, a sourced or measured shear correction factor, safety factor, and deflection limit. Record printer ID, immutable printer/process/filament profile hash, print orientation, infill, temperature and whether the effective section is solid or qualified for that profile. Obtain the hash from the matching immutable \`profileHash\` in \`workbench_manufacturing_profiles\`; register an inspected profile first if needed. A draft or imported profile hash identifies configuration only and does not qualify mechanical properties. Never infer strength, modulus, or allowable stress from slicer temperature, density, vendor, or material name. An unknown effective section or unconfirmed material/profile keeps the result conditional; changing profile or orientation makes the prior report stale. The tool calculates root bending, maximum rectangular-section shear, and bending plus Timoshenko shear deflection. A \`conditional\` result means only that this limited root screen did not exceed its supplied limits; it can never pass the complete tongue-and-groove joint. Groove bearing/splitting, contact distribution, stress concentrations and the mating part remain unchecked. If geometry, material direction, loading, or contact cannot be represented by these assumptions, ask focused questions or use a supported full-part analysis instead.

## Threaded receiver

Use \`threaded-receiver-axial-v1\` only after the worst-case axial demand on one receiver is known. Record the thread designation and pitch, actual engagement, fully formed engaged thread count, and three compatible allowable tensile loads: internal-thread stripping, external-thread stripping, and fastener tensile failure. \`plasticity_calculate_threaded_receiver_strength\` applies the safety factor once to the demand, reports utilization for every mode, and checks whether fastener tension occurs before thread stripping when that failure hierarchy is required. Nominal \`M5\`, pitch, or engagement alone never supplies a capacity. For a procured nut or threaded insert, use a controlled specified assembly load or dedicated matched test; an unqualified nominal shear-area calculation is unsupported. Parent-part pullout, boss splitting, transverse load, prying, preload generation, fatigue, temperature, and group distribution remain separate checks.

## Heat-set insert retention

Use \`heat-set-insert-retention-v1\` only with a worst-case demand for one installed insert and pullout/torque-out capacities tied to the exact insert ID, host material, printer profile, orientation, pocket dimensions and installation process. Manufacturer data for injection-moulded ABS or polycarbonate does not automatically qualify a printed PLA part. \`plasticity_calculate_heat_set_insert_retention\` applies the safety factor separately to axial pullout and torque. Simultaneous nonzero modes stay conditional because this method has no validated interaction equation. Check that the blind hole is at least the insert length plus two thread pitches and that the screw cannot bottom out.

## In-plane fastener group

Use \`fastener-group-elastic-in-plane-v1\` to resolve an in-plane force and moment to two or more distinct transfer points. Record every fastener coordinate, the force components, the point of application and any free moment in one common frame. For an existing Plasticity model, call \`plasticity_inspect_fastener_group\`, use its native coordinate evidence and binding, then call \`plasticity_verify_fastener_group_load\`; this re-reads the selected cylindrical faces and binds the saved report to their document revision and topology. On a supported rectangular face, also call \`plasticity_check_fastener_group_layout\` with the exact boundary face and qualified requirements. Supply separate circular envelopes for each relevant head, washer, nut or driver access diameter; preserve the source or user decision in \`basis\`. Without requirements the tool returns \`measured\`, never \`pass\`. A layout pass covers geometric spacing only. \`plasticity_distribute_fastener_group_load\` is only for an unbound caller-described scenario. Both calculation paths give each fastener an equal direct-force share and an elastic moment share proportional to its distance from the group centroid. They apply no safety factor. A \`calculated\` status means load distribution only; it is not a strength pass. When there is a traceable design-allowable shear load for each exact fastener configuration, pass every per-fastener value in \`shearCapacities\`; each value must already include the required safety factor. The optional result compares calculated elastic demand against those allowables for fastener shear only. It does not check plate bearing, tear-out, inserts, threads or complete joint strength. Without qualified allowables, report distribution only. Never infer capacity from nominal M size. Keep out-of-plane load, prying, member flexibility, unequal stiffness, preload, friction, clearance, slip and nonlinear redistribution explicit as unchecked. For a multi-hole plate, use \`plasticity_verify_fastener_group_plate_bearing\` only with exact opposed rectangular faces, a revision-bound group load, and directly traceable factored allowables. It checks per-hole projected bearing, and optionally a separately supplied external resultant across the minimum straight cut normal to local X or Y plus aligned per-hole two-plane edge tear-out. Do not infer external plate tension from bolt demands or use a local screen as a complete-joint pass. Angled/staggered fracture, compression buckling, shared-ligament interaction, bypass, plate bending, out-of-plane load, prying, inserts, threads and full-joint strength remain unchecked.

## Unsupported torsion

A nonzero moment about the section normal can be checked for an exact solid circle, concentric circular annulus, or uniform-thickness centered rectangular single-cell section. For the rectangular cell, \`THIN_WALLED_SINGLE_CELL_TORSION\` must be explicitly confirmed; otherwise the Bredt-Batho shear-flow result remains conditional. The tool reports median enclosed area, measured wall thickness, shear flow and nominal wall shear. It does not calculate twist, distortion, warping restraint or the interaction of simultaneous transverse shear and torsion. Other shapes remain unsupported; do not hide a resultant or turn a partial result into a pass.

## Stale face

Face IDs, revisions and topology signatures are one binding. If a manual edit, Undo, Redo or document switch changes any of them, discard the earlier face evidence. Re-inspect the exact current face and create a new immutable report after the user-requested action.

## Arbitrary critical plane

When the critical section lies inside the part, call \`plasticity_inspect_arbitrary_section\` with an explicit origin, normal and in-plane X direction. The tool builds and cancels a native temporary cut, checks that the document and Undo/Redo history did not change, and returns exact line/circle loops plus a session/document/revision-bound plane reference. When several plausible sections need comparing, use \`plasticity_inspect_arbitrary_sections\` with up to 32 explicit candidate planes, or \`plasticity_scan_arbitrary_sections\` to sample 2–32 evenly spaced stations along a caller-selected axis and interval. Both operate sequentially and refuse to return a mixed-revision result. They report geometry only; they do not select the load path, rank candidates or prove which plane is mechanically critical. For a stored report, pass a selected verified plane binding to \`plasticity_verify_section_strength\`; it re-cuts the same plane, replaces caller geometry with exact measured data, checks the section again before saving, and re-inspects it whenever report freshness is requested.

When a load and material scenario is ready, \`plasticity_scan_section_strength\` evaluates the same scenario at every explicit scan station using exact measured B-rep geometry, then stores one immutable aggregate report with the measured inputs, calculations and reasons for unsupported stations. Read it with \`plasticity_section_strength_scan_report\` before relying on it; freshness is checked against the live session, document, revision and body. The tool ranks only supported maximum single-mode utilizations. If any station is unsupported or lacks a utilization, the ranking is incomplete and has no global governing-station claim. Even a complete ranking omits unimplemented interactions, local stress concentration, supports and whole-part failure; the tool does not choose the scan direction or load path.

The aggregate scan report retains exact inputs and calculations, but the station response omits loops. To persist a separately CAD-bound report for one selected station, re-inspect that exact plane with \`plasticity_inspect_arbitrary_section\`, then pass the returned loops, frame and binding with the scenario to \`plasticity_verify_section_strength\`.

## Stale report

After a manual CAD edit, document switch, printer profile change, material change or orientation change, an earlier report is stale. Read the current native geometry and the current manufacturing inputs, create new evidence records and calculate a new report. Never relabel the old report as current.

These examples stop at analysis and CAD review. They never authorize or start a print.`;

export const STRENGTH_METHODS_RESOURCE = `# Versioned strength methods

- \`axial-rectangle-v1\` 1.0.0: nominal axial tension and elastic extension for one constant solid-equivalent rectangular member. Compression, buckling, holes, variable sections and local load introduction are excluded.
- \`cantilever-tip-rectangle-v1\` 1.0.0: nominal surface bending stress and Euler-Bernoulli tip deflection for one ideal rectangular cantilever with a tip load. Distributed loads, multiple loads, holes, variable sections, torsion, local load introduction and lateral instability outside the confirmed idealization are excluded.
- \`simply-supported-plate-uniform-pressure-v1\` 1.0.0: Navier-series centre deflection, centre moments and surface bending stresses for one homogeneous isotropic constant-thickness rectangular plate under static uniform normal pressure with all four edges ideally simply supported. The shorter-span/thickness ratio must be at least 10 and centre deflection may not exceed half the thickness. Other edge conditions, openings, ribs, bosses, curvature, local pressure, large-deflection membrane action and whole-enclosure behavior are excluded.
- \`euler-column-buckling-v1\` 1.0.0: ideal elastic Euler critical load for one straight, constant solid-equivalent rectangular column under centred axial compression. It uses the weak section axis, explicit effective-length factor K, Young's modulus and a sourced/measured elastic limit; it separately checks Euler buckling and nominal crushing against the compressive allowable after the safety factor. The method is unsupported when Euler critical stress exceeds the elastic limit. Eccentricity, initial curvature, inelastic/transition buckling, local or torsional modes, intermediate restraints, joints, anisotropy and code compliance are excluded.
- \`single-fastener-plate-v1\` 1.0.0: projected bearing, conservative two-plane loaded-edge shear-out and nominal net-section tension for one through fastener in an exact rectangular constant-thickness plate. The load direction selects the edge; exact opposed faces supply thickness, diameter, edge distances, width and side clearances. Separate sourced or measured bearing, shear and tensile limits are mandatory. The simple passport rejects e/d below 1.5 and keeps 1.5 ≤ e/d < 2 conditional. Fastener strength, preload, slip, clearance, fatigue, prying, pull-through, inserts, threads and fastener groups are excluded.
- \`fastener-member-v1\` 1.0.0: axial tension and direct shear for one explicitly loaded fastener using traceable tensile and shear effective areas and one or two equal shear planes. Combined nonzero loads use the NASA screening relation \`R_t² + R_s³ ≤ 1\` after explicit applicability confirmation. Fastener bending, slip, preload loss, load redistribution, thread/insert failure, joined-member failure, fatigue and critical-use qualification are excluded.
- \`tongue-root-transverse-v1\` 1.0.0: root-only elastic screening for one rectangular prismatic tongue idealized as a cantilever under a transverse point load. It reports root bending and maximum rectangular-section shear plus Euler-Bernoulli bending and Timoshenko shear tip deflection. Effective moduli, strength limits and shear correction factor require traceable, print-orientation-matched evidence; printer/process/filament profile hash, orientation, infill, temperature and effective-section qualification are part of the stored input. A passing root check is always reported as conditional and never validates a tongue-and-groove joint; groove contact, wall splitting, root concentration, mating-part strength and whole-joint safety are excluded.
- \`threaded-receiver-axial-v1\` 1.0.0: axial internal-thread stripping, external-thread stripping, and fastener tensile-failure screening from three explicit configuration-matched allowable loads. It verifies the requested failure hierarchy and never derives capacity from nominal M size. Procured nuts and inserts require a specified assembly allowable or matched dedicated test. Transverse load, prying, preload generation, parent-part or boss failure, fatigue, temperature and group distribution are excluded.
- \`heat-set-insert-retention-v1\` 1.0.0: independent axial pullout and torque-out screening for one installed heat-set insert using matched per-insert qualification capacities. Insert, host material, print profile, orientation, pocket and installation process are part of the immutable input. Combined pullout/torque interaction, transverse shear, boss failure, thread/screw failure, fatigue and multiple-insert load distribution are excluded.
- \`fastener-group-elastic-in-plane-v1\` 1.0.0: elastic distribution of an in-plane force and centroidal moment over two or more distinct, identical-stiffness fastener points on a rigid attachment. It reports the vector and magnitude at every fastener and the governing shear demand. This is load distribution only; fastener, insert and joined-member strength, out-of-plane load, prying, flexibility, preload, friction, clearance, slip and nonlinear redistribution are excluded.

- \`planar-section-resultants-v1\` 1.3.0: nominal axial and biaxial-bending normal stress on one exact planar line/circle boundary. Maximum direct shear is limited to a proven solid rectangle, solid circle or concentric circular annulus. Maximum elastic torsional shear also supports one exact uniform-thickness rectangular single cell with explicit thin-wall applicability confirmation. Each result records the selected formula family. Combined transverse shear and torsion, torsional twist, other noncircular torsion, deflection, buckling, local stress concentration, joints, fatigue and creep are excluded.

Every result lists missing inputs, assumptions, exclusions and unchecked scopes. A native B-rep dimension does not validate material properties, printer anisotropy, joints or loading.`;

export const STRENGTH_INTERLAYER_BASELINE_RESOURCE = `# Creality CR-PLA interlayer literature baseline

Checked 2026-09-26 against the accessible source pages and primary-paper records. Scope is one unfilled PLA material per part. This is a literature-screening reference, not a qualified material card, exact-process match, design allowable, or print approval.

Evidence verdict: an independent physical CR-PLA-associated layer-adhesion test reports two break loads (36.20 and 35.95 kg; mean 36.08 kg) on vertically printed coupons with a stated 4 × 4 mm minimum section. Assuming the chart's kg means kgf, calculated nominal coupon stresses are 22.19 and 22.03 MPa (mean 22.11 MPa). See the [test overview](https://www.mytechfun.com/video/123), [results chart](https://www.mytechfun.com/download/123/results-creality-filaments.png), and [specimen method](https://www.mytechfun.com/pla/). This is the closest public same-product layer-normal screen found, but it is non-peer-reviewed, has only two samples and incomplete batch/method/uncertainty provenance; it is a screening comparison, never an allowable or cohesive traction. A Creality-branded CR-PLA TDS separately reports Z-direction tensile strength of 6.63 MPa; another same-named TDS copy reports 8.63 MPa. A 2026 physical study also identifies Creality CR-PLA and reports printed-coupon tensile tests plus qualitative porosity/interlayer erosion after thermal cycling, but does not isolate layer-normal failure or measure crack growth. No CR-PLA DCB/ENF/MMB fracture test or full traction-separation law was found. None of these sources supports a reliable cohesive calculation. Physical layer delamination has also been observed in Creality Hyper PLA flexural coupons, but Hyper PLA is a different SKU and its nominal flexural stresses are not interface properties. Do not transfer them into a CR-PLA cohesive law.

## Creality product reference

The selected named starting product is Creality CR-PLA. Creality's product listing identifies CR-PLA but does not expose a downloadable mechanical-property dataset. A mirrored Creality CR-PLA Filament Technical Data Sheet, version 2.0 (2021-12), reports tensile strength of 31 MPa in X-Y and 6.63 MPa in Z (ISO 527 / GB/T 1040), plus elongation at break of 7.3% X-Y and 3.5% Z. Its example print conditions are 200 °C nozzle, 50 °C bed, 60 mm/s and 100% infill. Treat these as orientation-dependent manufacturer reference values only; the TDS does not provide a full stress-strain curve, scatter, confirmed specimen details, or this user's K1C process identity. The Z tensile number is not a measured cohesive peak traction and is not a layer-interface traction-separation curve.

Copies of same-named CR-PLA TDS documents disagree: another mirrored version reports 48–51 MPa X-Y and 8.63 MPa Z, while the selected v2.0 copy reports 31 MPa X-Y and 6.63 MPa Z; some product listings state 60 MPa without enough context to resolve specimen/process differences. Keep both reports separate, do not average them, and do not silently treat the lower value as a guaranteed minimum. Neither is an exact-process qualification, design allowable, cohesive peak traction, fracture energy or traction-separation curve.

Baseline decision: use Creality CR-PLA as the named reference material, but assign no numeric interlayer-strength property from public literature. If a user explicitly asks for comparisons, cite the MyTechFun loads of 36.20 / 35.95 kg and derived 22.19 / 22.03 MPa nominal coupon stresses (mean 22.11 MPa, assuming kgf and the stated 16 mm² section) only as a two-sample CR-PLA-associated vertical layer-adhesion screen with incomplete spool/batch traceability. Cite the 6.63 MPa TDS Z value and conflicting 8.63 MPa report separately. These are disconnected tests with conflicting/missing provenance, not a range, statistical interval or conservative bound; do not treat 6.63 MPa as a guaranteed lower bound. None is a delamination fracture property: never pass them to the cohesive solver or convert them into allowables. A reliable delamination calculation still requires exact-process physical interface curves, including crack-growth response, and observed interface failure.

## Physical CR-PLA tensile coupons on a Creality printer

Ilić et al. (MMA 2024) physically tested 27 tensile coupons made from 1.75 mm Creality CR-PLA on a Creality Ender 3 Pro. The paper says it used Cura's Ender 3 Pro / Generic PLA profile without changes, 210 °C nozzle, 50 °C bed, 60 mm/s, and three simultaneously printed coupons per infill condition. The reported mean tensile strengths were: Grid 30.0 MPa, Lines 30.2 MPa, Triangle 28.8 MPa, Cubic 30.1 MPa, Lighting 29.8 MPa, Gyroid 30.6 MPa, Zig-Zag 32.6 MPa at 60% infill; 100% infill 46.9 MPa; 100% wall 52.9 MPa. These are physically measured results on the named Creality filament, unlike the generic-PLA DCB study below, but they are not K1C/Creality Print results. The paper tabulates means but does not provide tabulated per-coupon results, standard deviations, confidence intervals, or raw numeric curves; it also does not establish Z-build orientation and varies infill architecture rather than measuring a layer-interface fracture law. Its preprocessing section calls the specimen ASTM D790-compliant, while its test section says ASTM D638; preserve this method-reporting inconsistency. Use the numbers only as a separately identified literature comparison, never as a guaranteed minimum, measured material card for the user's process, Z allowable, cohesive peak traction, or delamination calibration.

MyTechFun's public [Creality filament review](https://www.mytechfun.com/video/123) identifies CR-PLA among tested materials and includes a layer-adhesion test. Its downloadable [results chart](https://www.mytechfun.com/download/123/results-creality-filaments.png) reports two PLA layer-adhesion break loads, 36.20 and 35.95 kg (displayed mean 36.08 kg). The chart states a 4 × 4 mm minimum section; the author's [method description](https://www.mytechfun.com/pla/) says this specimen is printed vertically with maximum perimeters. Assuming kgf, the derived nominal coupon stresses are 22.19 and 22.03 MPa (mean 22.11 MPa). The chart labels the category only “PLA” and does not tie the individual coupons to a spool/batch. This is a useful same-product physical screen, with only two specimens and no reported test standard, machine calibration, uncertainty or batch traceability. It is not a direct local interface traction, design allowable or cohesive-law measurement; do not pass it into the solver.

Abebe et al. (2026, [DOI: 10.1016/j.polymer.2026.130199](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polymer.2026.130199)) identify their blue material as Creality CR-PLA and physically print spiral coupons; no spool-batch traceability is reported. They report tensile tests and optical/SEM observations. The article describes porous/rough CR-PLA fracture morphology and interlayer erosion/de-bonding after DSC thermal cycling. This is qualitative physical evidence of process-sensitive defects in the same named product, not a layer-normal coupon, delamination crack-growth test, fracture energy or interface constitutive law. Do not use its bulk tensile results or microscopy to assign a cohesive parameter.

## Direct Mode-I interlayer experiment on generic PLA

Lambiase et al. physically tested FDM PLA using double-cantilever-beam (DCB) specimens with a crack along an interlayer interface and measured Mode-I critical energy-release rate. The paper's abstract reports 0.75 kJ/m² for monodirectional 0° roads, 2.4 kJ/m² for monodirectional 90° roads, 2.0 kJ/m² for alternating 0°/90°, and 3.9 kJ/m² for alternating ±45°. Its conclusion separately gives approximately 0.74 kJ/m² for the longitudinal 0° case and approximately 2.0 kJ/m² for the other monodirectional case. Preserve this internal summary discrepancy; do not present a single exact 90° mean as settled without rechecking the full data/plots. Specimens used a 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.2 mm layers, 100% extrusion multiplier and five repetitions per deposition strategy; the authors describe the specimen as homogeneous. The filament is not identified as Creality CR-PLA, so these are generic-PLA sensitivity context only. The raw supporting data are available from the corresponding author on request; no source-backed per-specimen curve is available here.

For an explicitly requested, separate uncalibrated Mode-I sensitivity discussion, cite the study's reported generic-PLA values as approximately 0.74/0.75 kJ/m² (0°), 2.0/2.4 kJ/m² (90°, discrepancy between conclusion and abstract), 2.0 kJ/m² (alternating 0°/90°) and 3.9 kJ/m² (alternating ±45°). Do not submit these values to the calibrated solver or treat them as part allowables. The low value is a study average, not a guaranteed lower bound. These G_Ic figures alone cannot define cohesive peak traction, separation at failure, Mode-II/III response, or a Turon/mixed-mode law. No publicly accessible exact-process Creality CR-PLA DCB/ENF/MMB curve has been found in this research.

## Creality Hyper PLA physical flexural delamination evidence

Stanciu et al. (Polymers 2026, DOI: 10.3390/polym18050624) report physical tensile and ISO 178 flexural tests of a new/sealed spool of white Creality Hyper PLA on a Creality K1 Max. Flat ISO 527-2:2025 tensile coupons had five specimens per raster group; flexural coupons had six. They observed layer delamination in broken flexural specimens and reported nominal flexural stresses of 74 / 64 / 65 / 51 MPa for concentric / 0-90 / 45 / 90 groups. These are whole-coupon orientation/process results, not interface traction or fracture-energy values. Their flat tensile group means were 42.13 / 36.96 / 34.20 / 28.24 MPa for concentric / 0-90 / 45 / 90 raster. The setup used K1 Max, 0.4 mm nozzle, 220 °C nozzle, 60 °C bed, 100% infill, two walls and 300 mm/s; all tensile coupons were flat. This is a different Creality SKU and printer/process from CR-PLA / the user's K1C. Preserve it as a separate qualitative delamination and process-sensitivity reference only. It supplies no direct Z-normal strength, G_Ic/G_IIc, traction-separation curve or exact-process cohesive stiffness.

## Generic-PLA Z-normal physical tests (method/context only)

Eryildiz (2021, DOI: 10.26701/ems.881254) physically tested commercial PLA tensile coupons printed upright on a Creality Ender 3 Pro. The load was normal to the build layers; the paper reports interlayer fracture and a nominal coupon tensile strength of 35.52 MPa versus 55.49 MPa for the flat 0° condition (36% lower). Reported process: D638 Type IV, 0.4 mm nozzle, 0.2 mm layer height, 30% infill, three walls, 200 °C nozzle, 60 °C bed, 60 mm/s; Instron 8872 at 1 mm/min. The filament brand is unspecified, and replicate count/scatter are not reported. This is an observed Z-build coupon failure stress, not a conservative limit or isolated interface traction.

Perez et al. (2021, DOI: 10.1089/3dp.2020.0109) physically tested custom generic-PLA tensile specimens designed to isolate printed interfaces. The paper reports 22.8 MPa average tensile strength for the printed specimen and interface debonding. Its 33.75 MPa average interface strength was inferred by varying cohesive strength in FEM until response matched the physical tests; it was not measured directly as interface traction. Filament brand and a match to Creality Hyper PLA/CR-PLA are not established. Keep both numeric results as external context only; do not bind them to a Creality product or user's process.

## Source records

- Creality CR-PLA product page: https://www.crealitycloud.com/product/filament-resin/cr-pla-65d46152edcaa74b4d85e6a1 — confirms product identity; the page's product-data section reports no mechanical values.
- Mirrored CR-PLA TDS v2.0 (2021-12): https://assets.spoolscout.com/tds-sds/creality/tds/creality-cr-pla-tds-v2.0-2021-12.pdf — manufacturer-branded document mirror; values above are not independently measured on the user's equipment.
- Conflicting mirrored same-named CR-PLA TDS: https://www.vital3d.co.uk/_downloads/c987272aabbd74b34e71b1c5035d70ee — reports X-Y 48–51 MPa and Z 8.63 MPa; version metadata alone does not reconcile it with the selected copy.
- Ilić et al., “Effect of Infill Pattern on the Tensile Strength of FDM 3D Printed PLA Material,” 15th International Scientific Conference MMA 2024, DOI: https://doi.org/10.24867/MMA-2024-08-003; primary paper PDF: https://mma.ftn.uns.ac.rs/papers/MMA-2024-08-003.pdf — physical CR-PLA coupon series on Creality Ender 3 Pro, with the process and reporting limitations above.
- Abebe et al., “Analysis and characterization of influence of PLA printing parameters on 4D printing retention time under thermal stimulus,” Polymer 358 (2026), 130199, DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.polymer.2026.130199 — physically printed Creality CR-PLA spiral coupons, tensile testing and qualitative microscopy observations; no isolated layer-normal strength or fracture-mechanics curve.
- MyTechFun, “Creality filament review, part 2: mechanical tests (PLA, SILK, PETG, TPU, ABS, Carbon),” https://www.mytechfun.com/video/123; result chart: https://www.mytechfun.com/download/123/results-creality-filaments.png; PLA specimen method: https://www.mytechfun.com/pla/ — reports two vertical layer-adhesion coupon break loads and a 4 × 4 mm minimum area; associated review names CR-PLA among tested materials. Non-peer-reviewed, two specimens, incomplete batch and measurement provenance; the derived nominal stress is not a cohesive parameter.
- Lambiase et al., “Influence of the deposition pattern on the interlayer fracture toughness of FDM components,” The International Journal of Advanced Manufacturing Technology (2023), DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/s00170-023-12223-1 — primary DCB study and reported raster-dependent Mode-I results.
- Stanciu et al., “Experimental and Numerical Investigation of Mechanical Properties of Hyper Polylactic Acid (HPLA),” Polymers 18(5), 624 (2026), DOI: https://doi.org/10.3390/polym18050624 — physical Creality Hyper PLA tests and flexural delamination observations, with the transfer limits above.
- Eryildiz, “Effect of Build Orientation on Mechanical Behaviour and Build Time of FDM 3D-Printed PLA Parts: An Experimental Investigation,” DOI: https://doi.org/10.26701/ems.881254 — generic commercial PLA on Creality Ender 3 Pro; directly tested upright tensile coupons that failed between layers; replicate count is not stated.
- Perez et al., “Investigation of Interlayer Interface Strength and Print Morphology Effects in Fused Deposition Modeling 3D-Printed PLA,” DOI: https://doi.org/10.1089/3dp.2020.0109 — generic-PLA custom physical interface tensile tests plus an FEM-inferred cohesive strength; no Creality SKU.

## Use limits

Keep this literature record separate from \`plasticity_record_material_coupon_data\` and \`plasticity_record_material_interface_test\`. It must not satisfy exact printer/profile/layer-height matching, support a pass/fail verdict, or be represented as Creality- or K1C-qualified data. The currently implemented cohesive solver remains unavailable for a calibrated material result until physical same-material interface testing supplies traceable DCB, ENF and any required MMB response for the selected single-material process. Multi-material strength calculations are out of scope.`;

export const STRENGTH_RECOVERY_RESOURCE = `# Recovery

- After an MCP restart, read \`plasticity_construction_history\` before making CAD changes. After connecting to the explicit Plasticity window, read \`plasticity_construction_journal\` and compare \`durableSyncStatus\`, document token and revision. If the latest event is unknown or the document/revision diverged, inspect current status and selection; never replay or automatically undo it.
- A failed, timed-out or interrupted request is terminal. Read it with \`plasticity_strength_request\`; use a new request ID for a deliberate new attempt.
- After a Codex timeout or cancellation, do not assume whether a partial answer was produced and do not retry automatically.
- If Plasticity changes during geometry inspection, discard the collected topology and inspect the new revision explicitly.
- If a report is stale, retain it as history and create a new verified report. Do not overwrite stored reports.
- If no Plasticity session is connected, calculations remain available as labelled scenarios; native verification waits for an explicit window connection.`;

export function strengthPrompt(task?: string): string {
  return `Use the Plasticity strength workflow for this task: ${task?.trim() || "the user's current technical part"}.

${STRENGTH_CORE_WORKFLOW}

Start with evidence and applicability. Use Codex analysis only to extract observations, find missing facts and suggest a supported method. Use deterministic calculation tools for numbers. Treat ordinary calculations as scenarios. Native verification may bind only the geometry that the corresponding tool actually measures; for tongue-root work, plasticity_verify_tongue_root_strength binds only root width and thickness to an exact native rectangular cross-section, while load and material remain scenario evidence. For plate work, establish net pressure and all four edge conditions before selecting a method. For plate-around-hole work, identify the loaded edge from the real in-plane force direction and use separate traceable bearing, shear and tensile limits. For fastener-member work, identify the actual shear plane, effective areas, one-fastener loads, grade allowables and applicable preload before calculating. For tongue-root work, inspect a plane normal to the lever arm, reject any section that is not one axis-aligned rectangle without holes, and never report the component screen as whole-joint approval. For a threaded receiver, require actual engagement, fully formed thread count, and matched internal-strip, external-strip, and fastener tensile allowables; never use M size as capacity. For heat-set inserts, require a worst-case per-insert demand and a configuration-matched pullout/torque qualification; treat a different plastic or process as unconfirmed or mismatched. For fastener groups, record exact transfer points and the complete in-plane force, point of application and free moment; treat the distribution as demand input to later component checks. For planar-section work, identify the critical plane and explain why it represents the load path; use an arbitrary native section when no existing face matches it. Report unsupported conditions and unchecked components plainly. Nominal section, plate or fastener stress is not whole-part validation. Do not authorize CAD changes or printing from an analysis result.`;
}
